{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:ad3e2f64f5c9ad49ddcfab416b3c674cad4df95aecbbe3122d528ea655a776f2","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.95},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.65}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THE ANCHOR IS WRONG BY ~85bp AND THAT IS MY HEADLINE FINDING. The brief anchors on US 10y TIPS ~2.60% (Oct 30 2026), but the question asks for the GDP-WEIGHTED G7 real yield. Pricing it today on PPP GDP weights (US .52, JP .11, DE .10, UK .07, FR .07, IT .06, CA .05) with current national real yields (US 2.60, JP ~0.35, DE ~0.60, FR ~0.90, IT ~1.40, UK ~1.30, CA ~1.50) gives ~1.76%, not 2.60%. Any panelist who anchors on 2.60 and adjusts will be ~0.8pp too high. Worse, the WEIGHTING BASIS is genuinely unspecified: the locked accounting basis is 2025 USD PPP, but a yield is defined in local nominal terms, so the weights do not inherit the lock. On MARKET-FX GDP weights the US share rises to ~0.64 and the same observation prices at ~2.07% — a +31bp artefact, far above my 3pp flag threshold. I resolve on PPP weights per the lock and report the 31bp as the dominant definitional risk.\nOTHER MEASUREMENT ARTEFACTS >3pp. (i) TIPS BREAKEVEN vs INFLATION SWAP: US 10y CPI swaps trade ~20-35bp ABOVE the TIPS breakeven (TIPS liquidity premium plus deflation floor). Using swaps LOWERS the measured US real yield by 20-35bp; at .52 weight that is 10-18bp on the aggregate. The question says \"10y inflation swap / TIPS breakeven\" as if interchangeable. They are not. (ii) JAPAN: the JGBi market and yen inflation-swap market are thin; the Japanese BEI is biased LOW by illiquidity, so Japan's measured real yield is biased HIGH by 20-40bp (x .11 = +3-4bp). (iii) ITALY/FRANCE/GERMANY all get the SAME euro-area HICP swap, so Italy's measured \"real yield\" embeds the BTP-Bund credit spread as though it were a real rate (+5bp on the aggregate). (iv) CONCEPTUAL: nominal = r* + real term premium + E[pi] + inflation risk premium. Subtracting a breakeven (= E[pi] + IRP) returns r* + real term premium only if the IRPs match; they do not, and in stress the TIPS-implied real rate overstates the true real rate by 10-25bp. (v) \"AVERAGED over 2032-2036\": an arithmetic mean over five years of a non-mean-reverting level. This is level-dominated, kills the spike tails, and NARROWS the distribution substantially versus the point-in-time question a naive reader answers. Expected net artefact on the measured aggregate vs the conceptual one: ~+10bp.\nREQUIRED DECOMPOSITION: CQ4 = r* + real term premium - repression wedge.\nr* (G7 PPP-weighted, ~0.75%, range 0.3-1.3): UP from boomer life-cycle DISSAVING (+20-30bp vs 2015-19) and from physical-AI/grid capex (+10-20bp only — see my Identity-2 audit: AI-Q3's own TWh range implies $20-60B/yr of incremental OECD+China grid capex, ~0.1-0.3% of bloc gross fixed capital formation, an order of magnitude too small to move a global saving-investment balance; the crowding must run through interconnection queues, transformer/turbine supply and electricity PRICES, not loanable funds). DOWN from precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty, cognitive-labour income compression (CQ5/AI-Q5 channel), and the persistent Chinese household saving surplus with no domestic investment outlet (-20 to -30bp). Japan and euro r* well below US, which is why the G7-weighted r* is low.\nREAL TERM PREMIUM (+1.10%, range 0.4-2.0) — THIS CARRIES MOST OF MY VARIANCE, and it is the correct home for Section 2A's \"(r-g) worsens by ~+1.0pp EVEN WHERE a frictionless r* eases.\" That claim is a term-premium-and-fiscal-arithmetic claim, not an r* claim, and I honour the distinction: my r* central is only ~0.75% while my term premium does the work. Drivers: rollover VOLUME at >100% global debt/GDP (142% US, 127% China by 2031) with net interest to ~3% of GDP; eroding sovereign convenience yields; levered hedge-fund basis trade as a fragile marginal buyer whose capacity is balance-sheet- and repo-haircut-contingent; reduced central-bank duration absorption; and an underrated DEMOGRAPHIC channel on the long end — closed DB schemes reaching full funding and buying out REMOVE a price-insensitive long-duration bid (+10-25bp), i.e. aging raises the term premium through the asset side, not just the fiscal side.\nREPRESSION WEDGE (-20bp expected, fat left tail): I treat repression as a real policy option, not a curiosity. P(at least one G7 running materially repressive policy for a sustained part of 2032-36) = ~35% (Japan most likely, then Italy/UK via Eurosystem or regulatory mandate; US least likely but largest if it happens). Expected wedge 0.35 x ~60bp = -20bp; conditional on the US adopting it, -100bp or more, which is what builds my left tail.\nCENTRAL: 0.75 + 1.10 - 0.20 = 1.65% conceptual, +10bp measurement artefact = 1.75% measured. Path-wise this is roughly FLAT versus today's PPP-weighted 1.76%, because two moves cancel: Japan's real yield rises 40-80bp on BoJ normalization while the US mean-reverts DOWN from 2.60 toward 1.9-2.1 as the 2026 term premium normalizes. P10 0.95 (low r* + repression + post-bust precautionary saving). P90 2.65 (fiscal dominance expressed entirely through the term premium, no repression, strong capex pull); the five-year averaging is what keeps P90 below 3%.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.381Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD switches its headline GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate to an ex-Turkiye basis (or Turkiye core settles below 5%), so that the resolving series no longer carries the +0.25-0.60pp wedge.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My core-inflation artefact claim is spent: CQ2's first conjunct falls ~8-12pp (CQ2 to ~25), CQ6-C falls ~5-6pp and CQ6-D rises ~5-6pp. The direction of the whole inflation leg of my view reverses from artefact-driven to fundamentals-driven.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey 2026 (publication due ~2029) reports median gross hourly earnings by NACE section for 25+ OECD members with section Q's real median rising FASTER than J+K+M over 2022-2026 — i.e. the entrant-composition drag I claim does not appear in the data.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central CQ1 mechanism is falsified: conjunct 2 rises from 13% toward ~28%, CQ1 moves from 8 to ~17. Also weakens hypothesis plank (a)'s critique and lifts CQ6-A by ~2pp via the broad-wage-gains channel.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield (PPP weights, national breakeven basis) prints below 1.00% for four consecutive quarters with NO explicit yield cap, mandated sovereign-holding rule, or central-bank purchase programme sized >3% of GDP announced in any G7 member.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My \\\"term premium carries the variance, repression is a 35% option\\\" decomposition fails and an easing frictionless r* dominates instead: CQ4 P50 drops ~50bp to ~1.25 with P90 down to ~2.1, CQ6-D rises ~6pp, CQ6-C falls ~4pp, and CQ5's first conjunct falls ~8pp as the level-dependence channel for MOVE disappears.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Any G7 sovereign announces an explicit 10-year yield ceiling, a regulatory minimum sovereign-debt holding for banks or insurers, or a central-bank \\\"market functioning\\\" purchase programme exceeding 3% of GDP.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The repression wedge is realized rather than probability-weighted: CQ4 P50 down ~35bp and P90 down ~60bp (the top tail is truncated by policy, not by economics); CQ5's MOVE conjunct down ~8pp because capped yields suppress bp-denominated rate vol; CQ6-C rises ~3pp on the financial-repression clause in its own definition.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs AND G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominals fall by >=50bp during that window (i.e. the bond hedge WORKS), while the trailing MOVE average since Jan 2027 is still below 110.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5's most likely qualifying episode is consumed by a non-qualifying case and the remaining window is short: CQ5 drops from 23 to ~9-11. It also confirms my claim that the modal large drawdown is a disinflationary AI-capex bust, which pulls CQ6 mass from C toward D by ~4pp and lowers CQ4 P50 by ~20bp.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'rstar'."}}